event_names/558"} "Event type"]] [:h1 {:class "event_title"} "400 metres Freestyle, Men" [:small {}]] [:table {:class "biodata"} [:tbody {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Date"] [:td {} "7 –  9 August 1928"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Status"] [:td {} "Olympic"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Location"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/venues/288"} "Zwemstadion, Amsterdam"]]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Participants"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/events/4843"} "26 from 17 countries"]]]]] [:div {:class "description"} [:p {} [:a {:href "/athletes/51611"} "Johnny Weissmuller"] " of the USA was the champion " [:a {:href "/results/4741"} "four years earlier in Paris"] ", but did not defend his title in Amsterdam. The USA was still well represented by a trio of top class swimmers, Argentina by the New York-based " [:a {:href "/athletes/44989"} "Alberto Zorrilla"] ", and the two other medalists from Paris, " [:a {:href "/athletes/50360"} "Arne Borg"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/45177"} "Andrew “Boy” Charlton"] ", returned with strong chances to upgrade their medals to gold."] "\n\n" [:p {} "It was Borg who recorded the fastest times in both heat and semi-final, as the fancied contenders all the made their way to the final. Borg and Charlton were old rivals and seemed more intent on watching each other than the other swimmers and, although the two men fought for the lead throughout the race, Zorrilla was able to move through from an outside draw and win Argentina’s first Olympic swimming championship by two clear seconds. The Argentine was an all-round sportsman, also excelling at rowing, boxing and athletics. His plan to defend his title in " [:a {:href "/results/4946"} "Los Angeles"] " was thwarted by illness."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Competitor"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {} "R1"] [:th {} "SF"] [:th {} "Final"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/44989"} "Alberto Zorrilla"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countri